    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were attacked from the flank and rear while marching in column, possibly in poor visibility, and had no chance to form even a rudimentary fighting line.</li><li>Of about 25,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, 15,000 were killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>'s count, and the 6,000 at the head of the column who escaped were surrounded later in the day and surrendered.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> answered the defeat by electing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Fabius Maximus</a> dictator, who avoided pitched battle; the next year's consuls fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The destruction of an entire army by an ambush laid by another army is widely considered a unique occurrence; Basil Liddell Hart called it the greatest ambush in history.</p></aside>
    </section>

