    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">219 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> besieges, captures and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> declares war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> after crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> by an unguarded route and march into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, plundering</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> ambushes Flaminius's army on the north shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A few days later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> wipe out the cavalry of the second Roman army under Geminus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a> Verrucosus is elected dictator and adopts a strategy of avoiding pitched battle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Paullus and Varro, elected consuls, offer battle and are defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">202 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> is defeated at Zama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> agrees to a peace that strips it of most of its territory and power</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-lake-trasimene" data-v="03faaf27d6" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Carthaginians lie hidden behind the hills</b> After dark Hannibal moved his army behind the hills and placed it on the reverse slopes: cavalry in the west, Gauls next, and the African and Iberian infantry in the east near the camp. The Romans, who could see the camp, set off along the lakeshore.</li><li><b>The head of the column meets the easternmost Carthaginians</b> The Romans marched east in a thick morning mist, by the ancient accounts, with no cavalry scouts sent ahead. The leading men made contact with the most easterly Carthaginians and the signal to advance was given.</li><li><b>The Carthaginians attack from the flank and rear</b> The whole Carthaginian force came down from the hills against the column's flank and rear. The Romans could not form even a rudimentary fighting line, and some fled while others gathered in groups to face the enemy on all sides.</li><li><b>The trapped Romans fight on and are beaten down</b> Some Roman groups fought hard, especially in the centre, where the attacking Gauls suffered heavy casualties. After three hours the trapped Romans collapsed, and men who went into the lake were cut down or drowned.</li><li><b>The 6,000 at the front push out through the defile</b> The trap did not close on the 6,000 Romans at the head of the column. They pushed out through the defile against little opposition and marched on.</li><li><b>The 6,000 are surrounded and surrender</b> Later in the day pursuing Carthaginians surrounded the 6,000 Romans. They surrendered to a force under Maharbal on the promise of being disarmed and freed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca.jpg" alt="Hamilcar Barca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amilcare_Barca_(Ritratto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Max Karl Baldamus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> was fought from 264 to 241 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the two main powers of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, chiefly over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and its waters, and ended in Carthaginian defeat. Five years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and turned it into a quasi-monarchical, autonomous territory ruled by the Barcids, which gave Carthage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, agricultural wealth and manpower. Hamilcar ruled as viceroy until 228 BCE, followed by his son-in-law Hasdrubal and then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in 221 BCE. In 219 BCE Hannibal besieged, captured and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, a city with a treaty of association with Rome, and Rome declared war.</p><p>Hannibal marched north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">New Carthage</a> in May 218 BCE and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in October, arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> with 20,000 infantry, 6,000 cavalry and an unknown number of the 37 elephants with which he had left Iberia. The consul Publius Scipio was beaten and wounded at the Ticinus. The army of Sempronius Longus, brought north from Sicily, was lured by Numidian cavalry onto ground of Hannibal's choosing at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, where most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were killed or captured, though 10,000 under Sempronius fought their way to Placentia.</p><p>Rome chose Gnaeus Geminus and Gaius Flaminius as consuls for 217 BCE, recruited further legions, reinforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and Sicily, built a fleet of 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a> and set up supply depots at Ariminum and Arretium. It fielded twenty-two legions that year, ten more than in 218 BCE.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Lake Trasimene&#8221; cites where it covers Pre-war, Carthage invades Italy, Prelude:</p><ul><li>John F. Lazenby, Hannibal's War: A Military History of the Second Punic War (Aris &amp; Phillips, 1998)</li><li>John Lazenby, The First Punic War: A Military History (Stanford University Press, 1996)</li><li>Dexter Hoyos, Mastering the West: Rome and Carthage at War (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Roman army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> was Flaminius's, stationed at Arretium: four legions, two Roman and two made up of allies, about 25,000 men in all. A legion traditionally had 4,200 infantry and 300 cavalry, of whom about 1,200 were javelin-armed skirmishers, the velites, and the rest heavy infantry with body armour, a large shield and a short thrusting sword. Allied legions had a larger complement of cavalry. The second Roman army, under Geminus, was on the Adriatic coast, so that the two could block an advance into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> usually recruited foreigners. North Africans, often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, supplied close-order infantry fighting in a tight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, javelin skirmishers and cavalry of two kinds. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> provided experienced infantry who charged ferociously but had a reputation for breaking off if a fight was protracted, and Balearic slingers were specialists. The numbers are not known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> with 31,000 infantry and 11,000 cavalry, and was then heavily reinforced by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>. It is usually assumed that more than 50,000 fought at Trasimene, and in any event the Carthaginian army was considerably larger than the Roman.</p><p>Hannibal chose the ground. The road ran along the north shore and then turned south through a defile, with low hills to the north that came closer to the lake towards the east. The Carthaginians camped, in full view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, where the hills were closest to the lake. At night he marched his troops behind the hills to the reverse slopes: cavalry furthest west, the north-Italian Gauls to their east and the African and Iberian infantry furthest east, near the camp. Modern historians place most of the light infantry round the defile or reinforcing the Gauls in the centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Flaminius, commanding at Arretium, learned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> had bypassed him and set off in pursuit. Adrian Goldsworthy points out that the army passed through devastated territory and would have felt humiliation, and that landowning officers and small farmers in the legions would have taken the destruction as a provocation. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> expected an easy victory, and they followed so fast that they could not reconnoitre properly, closing to less than a day's march behind. On 20 June the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> passed Cortona and marched along the north shore of the lake.</p><p>On the morning of 21 June the Romans set off very early and marched east along the lake, probably in three parallel columns, with a screen of light infantry in front. Flaminius sent out no cavalry scouts for distant reconnaissance, which Roman armies of the time rarely did. Ancient accounts speak of a thick morning mist; some modern historians suggest it was invented or exaggerated to excuse the Romans' unreadiness. The leading Romans met the easternmost Carthaginians and the signal to advance was given, possibly by trumpets. Some ancient accounts say the Romans heard it on their flank and rear but could not see the enemy.</p><p>The Romans had no chance to form even a rudimentary line. Some fled, others clustered in groups ready to fight on all sides, and the fugitives and many groups were quickly cut down or captured. In the centre other groups fought hard, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> suffered heavy casualties before beating them down after three hours. Polybius says Flaminius was surprised and gave no effective leadership; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> has him active and valiant in trying to rally the army before a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, Ducarius, killed him. Men tried to swim the lake and drowned.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Lake Trasimene&#8221; cites where it covers Setting the ambush, Springing the trap:</p><ul><li>Brian Todd Carey, Hannibal's Last Battle: Zama &amp; the Fall of Carthage (Pen &amp; Sword, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The trap did not close on the 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the head of the column, possibly those most prepared for battle. They pushed out of the defile against little opposition and marched on. Later in the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> under Maharbal surrounded them and they surrendered on a promise of being disarmed and freed, 'with a garment apiece' according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> disapproved and applied this only to the allied captives, selling the Romans into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Many Carthaginian infantry, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, took captured Roman armour.</p><p>The sources are unclear on the fate of the roughly 25,000 Romans engaged. Fabius Pictor has 15,000 killed and 10,000 scattered; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> 15,000 killed and most of the rest captured. For Carthaginian losses Polybius gives 1,500 killed, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, and Livy 2,500 killed on the day and many who later died of wounds. A few days later the whole 4,000-strong cavalry of Geminus's army, scouting ahead, was surprised: nearly 2,000 were killed in the first clash and the rest were surrounded and captured next day. Geminus withdrew his infantry to Ariminum.</p><p>Roman prisoners were badly treated, but captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> allies were treated well and many were freed, in the hope that they would speak well of Carthage and defect. The Carthaginians went on through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> to the Adriatic, plundering and killing captured adult men, the Gauls especially brutally, and then marched south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> panicked and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a> Verrucosus dictator.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from ancient historians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, usually reliable, wrote that Flaminius was completely surprised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, openly pro-Roman, wrote two centuries later and otherwise paints a poor picture of Flaminius but has him rally his men. Fabius Pictor, a contemporary annalist and senator, gave a casualty figure. They disagree on what became of the 25,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and Polybius and Livy give different Carthaginian losses.</p><p>Historians dispute some details. The shoreline has changed since 217 BCE. Some doubt the thick mist reported by ancient accounts. The positions of the Carthaginian light infantry and the Romans' marching order are modern reconstructions. The Carthaginian numbers are not known and can only be approximated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae.jpg" alt="Battle of Cannae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Death_of_Paulus_Aemilius_at_the_Battle_of_Cannae_(Yale_University_Art_Gallery_scan).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Trumbull, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Toni Ñaco del Hoyo describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> as one of the three great military calamities <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> suffered in the first three years of the war, the others being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Fabius Maximus</a>'s strategy of avoiding pitched battle, relying on low-level harassment to wear the invader down while Rome rebuilt its strength, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> largely free to ravage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> for the next year. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> then ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and elected the more aggressive Paullus and Varro, who offered battle at Cannae in 216 BCE, where some 70,000 Romans were killed or captured; Richard Miles calls it Rome's greatest military disaster. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> campaigned in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for a further 13 years.</p><p>In 204 BCE Publius Cornelius Scipio, the son of the Scipio wounded at the Ticinus, invaded the Carthaginian homeland, defeated the Carthaginians in two major battles and won the allegiance of the Numidian kingdoms. Hannibal was recalled and decisively defeated at Zama in October 202 BCE, and Carthage lost most of its territory and power.</p><p>Later military writers have treated Trasimene as exceptional. Basil Liddell Hart wrote that Hannibal planned and executed the greatest ambush in history. Theodore Dodge notes it is the only instance in history of lying in ambush with the whole of a large army, and Robert O'Connell writes that it was the only time an entire large army was effectively swallowed and destroyed by such a manoeuvre.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 21 June 217 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> ambushed Gaius Flaminius's Roman army of about 25,000 on the north shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a>, and nearly all of it was killed or captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Fabius Maximus</a> dictator, and the war went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> and Zama.</em></p>

